Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT Template — Templates (@stxt.template)
	Metadata:
		Author: Joan Costa Mombiela
		Author: Claude Fable 5
		Revision: Joan Costa Mombiela
		Revision: Claude Fable 5
		Version: 1.0
		Last modif: 2026-08-31
		
	Header: @STXT@ Template
	
	Subheader: 1. Introduction
	
	Content >>
		This document is **STXT-TEMPLATE-SPEC**; the other specifications cite it by that name.
		
		This document defines the specification of the **STXT Template** language, a mechanism for describing semantic rules
		(structure, types, cardinalities, and allowed values) applicable to @STXT@ documents.
		
		A **template**:
		
		* Is an @STXT@ document whose namespace is `@stxt.template`.
		* Is associated with a **target namespace**, analogously to a schema.
		* Describes the **expected structure** through a `Structure >>` block with simplified syntax.
		* Can be compiled into a semantically equivalent `@stxt.schema` document within the subset expressible by templates.
		
		Relationship with `@stxt.schema`:
		
		* An implementation **MAY** support only schemas, only templates, or both.
		* If both exist (schema and template) for the same target namespace,
		  a conforming implementation **SHOULD** establish a consistent prioritization criterion.
		  When definitions are discovered on the file system, that criterion is fixed by
		  [*STXT-DISCOVERY-SPEC*](stxt-discovery-ref): the definition from the nearest level wins, and two
		  definitions at the same level are an error.
		* For a specific validation, an implementation **MUST** use a single effective semantic source: either schema or template.
		* STXT core **MUST NOT** impose semantics: templates are an optional layer, after parsing the @STXT@ document.
		

	Subsubheader: 1.1 Version of this specification

	Content >>
		This specification carries its own version number in the `Version` field of its `Metadata`,
		**independent** of the other @STXT@ specifications, and follows the same change policy as
		[*STXT-SPEC* §1.1](stxt-core-ref#s1-1): `major.minor`, major if something valid stops being
		valid or changes meaning, minor if something is only added.
		It depends on [*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC*](stxt-schema-ref): every template compiles to an equivalent
		schema, and requires *STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC* 1.0 or later.

	Subheader: 2. Terminology
	
	Content >>
		The keywords **"MUST"**, **"MUST NOT"**, **"SHOULD"**, **"SHOULD NOT"**, and **"MAY"** must be interpreted according to **RFC 2119** and **RFC 8174**: they carry that meaning only when they appear in uppercase, as here.
		
		Terms such as *node*, *indentation*, *namespace*, *inline*, and *`>>` block* retain their meaning in [*STXT-SPEC*](stxt-core-ref).
		
		Additional definitions:
		
		* **Target namespace**: the @STXT@ namespace to which the template applies (for example `com.example.docs`).
		* **Node template**: an entry within the `Structure >>` block that defines a node (and optionally its type/cardinality and its children).
		* **Type**: a semantic validation label (for example `TEXT`, `DATE`, `ENUM`) equivalent to the types in @STXT@ Schema.
		* **Cardinality**: a rule that defines how many times a child node may appear relative to its parent.
		
	Subheader: 3. Relationship between @STXT@ and Template
	
	Content >>
		Validation through templates occurs **after** @STXT@ parsing:
		
		1. Parsing the @STXT@ document into a hierarchical structure.
		2. Resolving the effective namespace of each node.
		3. Selecting the corresponding template for the target namespace.
		4. Applying template rules (structure, cardinality, types, values).
		
		An implementation **MAY** apply rules during parsing, provided that this validation remains **loosely coupled** to the base parser.
		
	Subheader: 4. General Structure of a Template
	
	Content >>
		A template document **MUST** have as its root node:
		
		`Template (@stxt.template): <target_namespace>`
		
		The template **MUST** contain exactly one `Structure` node in block form (`>>`).
		
	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.docs
			Structure >>
				Document (com.example.docs):
					Title: (1)
					Body: (1) TEXT
			Description >>
				Document: Basic document with a title and a body
					
	Content >>
		Rules:
		
		* The root node `Template` **MUST** belong to the namespace `@stxt.template`.
		* `<target_namespace>` **MUST** be a valid namespace according to [*STXT-SPEC*](stxt-core-ref) (namespaces section).
		* The template document **MAY** include at most one `Description` node, with per-node descriptions ([section 12](#s12)).
		* There can only be **one effective template** per target namespace in a specific validation (see [section 5](#s5)).
		
	Subheader: 5. One template per target namespace
	
	Content >>
		For each logical namespace:
		
		* There **MUST NOT** be more than one effective template simultaneously.
		* If there are multiple candidate templates for the same namespace, the implementation **SHOULD** have a clear,
		  stable, and deterministic criterion for deciding which one applies.
		* For a specific validation, an implementation **MUST NOT** apply multiple templates at once to the same target namespace.
		
	Subheader: 6. `Structure >>` Block
	
	Content >>
		The `Structure >>` block defines a **tree of node templates** using indentation, where:
		
		* Each non-empty line defines a **node template**.
		* Indentation defines child nodes (just like @STXT@), but inside `Structure >>` a data document is not being described,
		  but rather an expected structure.
		* The indentation rules of the base @STXT@ document apply to the template document outside the block.
		* The content of `Structure >>` is interpreted through the template grammar defined in this document.
		* The hierarchy within `Structure >>` **MUST** be built with the same consecutive indentation model as in [*STXT-SPEC*](stxt-core-ref).
		
	Content >>
		**Top level and defined nodes.** The top-level lines of `Structure` do not have
		special "root" semantics: they are node templates like any other, and their nesting defines
		their children. Each line of `Structure` (at any level) defines or references a node of the
		corresponding namespace, exactly like a `Node` in a schema.
		
	Subsubheader: 6.1 Recommended style convention
	
	Content >>
		For readability, it is recommended:
		
		* 1 tab per level.
		* A single space between components.
		* Maintain consistency in names (use the same capitalization/diacritics as in real documents).
		
	Subsubheader: 6.2 Syntax of a `Structure` line
	
	Content >>
		Each line of the `Structure >>` block has the form:
		
		`<NodeName> [<NamespaceOverride>] ":" [<RuleSpec>]`
		
		where:
		
		* `<NodeName>` is the node name (human text; normalized according to [*STXT-SPEC*](stxt-core-ref), [section 4.3](stxt-core-ref#s4-3), for comparison).
		* `<NamespaceOverride>` is optional and has the form `(<namespace>)` or `(@special.namespace)` and defines the **effective** namespace of that template node.
		* `":"` is mandatory. It is not required by the core (the content of a `>>` block is opaque text to it), but by the **template grammar**, for visual consistency with @STXT@ syntax.
		* `<RuleSpec>` is optional and includes, in this logical order:
		  1. Optional cardinality (in parentheses).
		  2. Optional reference to a node through `@Node Name`, or alternatively a type.
		  3. Optional values if the type is `ENUM`.
		
		Examples:
		
	Code >>
		Title:
		Title: (1)
		Body: (1) TEXT
		Color: (?) ENUM [red, green, blue]
		Body Content: (?) @Body Content
		Metadata (org.example.meta): (0,1)
		
	Content >>
		Notes:
		
		* If `<RuleSpec>` is omitted entirely, default cardinality and default type are assumed ([sections 7](#s7) and [8](#s8)).
		* A template node may have children if its effective type allows them. The default type `INLINE` allows children. Types that forbid children are defined in [section 8](#s8).
		* A reference `@Node Name` **MUST NOT** be combined with an explicit type on the same line.
		
	Subsubheader: 6.3 Parsing rules for `Structure >>`
	
	Content >>
		A template parser **MUST**:
		
		1. Read the `Structure >>` block as a sequence of lines already canonicalized by @STXT@ core: block level removed and right trim per line ([*STXT-SPEC*](stxt-core-ref), [section 10.2](stxt-core-ref#s10-2)).
		2. Ignore empty lines.
		3. Compute hierarchy by indentation applying the rules of [*STXT-SPEC*](stxt-core-ref) ([section 8](stxt-core-ref#s8)): on each line, only tabs (1 tab = 1 level) or only spaces in multiples of 4, **with no mixing on the same line**, and with consecutive levels, without jumps.
		4. For each line, parse:
		   * Node name + optional namespace `(ns)` if present.
		   * The mandatory `:` character.
		   * The optional rule specification (`RuleSpec`).
		
		5. Resolve the effective namespace of each line.
		6. Resolve `@Node Name` references according to [section 6.4](#s6-4).
		
		A template parser **MUST** fail if a line does not contain `:`.
	
	Subsubheader: 6.4 Definition, reference, and recursion

	Content >>
		Templates distinguish between:
		
		* **Local node definition**: appearance of a node whose effective namespace belongs to the template's target namespace, with its complete `RuleSpec` (type, values, children).
		* **Local node reference**: reuse of a local definition through `@Node Name`.
		* **External reference**: appearance of a node whose effective namespace belongs to another namespace.
		
		Rules for definition and reference:
		
		* A local node **MUST NOT** be defined more than once per pair `canonical name + effective namespace`.
		* If a local node appears again elsewhere in `Structure`, it **MUST** be done through a `@Node Name` reference.
		* A `@Node Name` reference **MAY** override cardinality, but **MUST NOT** redefine type, `ENUM` values, or children.
		* The canonical name of the line and the canonical name of the `@Node Name` reference **MUST** match. A reference with a different name from its line **MUST** cause a template error (there is no renaming: `@` marks "reappearance of the same node", not "pointer to another").
		* Nodes from other namespaces **MUST NOT** be defined locally within the current template.
		* An external node **MAY** appear multiple times, but that line **MUST NOT** declare anything other than the cardinality (see [section 10](#s10)).
		
	Content >>
		**Recursion.** A `@Node Name` reference **MUST** point to:
		
		* a **previous** local definition (already closed) in the same template, **or**
		* an **ancestor node whose definition is open** at that point, **including the very node being defined**.
		
		This second option enables recursive structures: a node may be declared as a child of itself
		or of an ancestor. To resolve the reference only the node's **identity** is needed
		(name + namespace + type), which is already available on its definition line; it is not necessary
		for its list of children to be complete.
		
		Since writing `Structure` is equivalent to a depth-first traversal of the node graph, this rule
		covers any dependency graph: self-recursion, recursion through ancestors, and mutual
		recursion.
		
		Example (self-recursion):
		
	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.doc
			Structure >>
				Document (com.example.doc):
					Title: (1)
					Section: (*)
						Title: (1) @Title
						Text: (?) TEXT
						Section: (*) @Section
		
	Content >>
		In this example, the last line, `Section: (*) @Section`, declares `Section` as a child of itself,
		referencing the ancestor `Section` whose definition is open. `Title: (1) @Title`
		reuses the previous local definition of `Title`.
		
		Example (mutual recursion `A`/`B`):
		
	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.tree
			Structure >>
				A (com.example.tree):
					B: (*)
						A: (?) @A
		
	Subheader: 7. Cardinalities
	
	Content >>
		Cardinality applies **per instance of the parent node**, counting only direct children that match by:
		
		* Canonical node name (according to [*STXT-SPEC*](stxt-core-ref), [section 4.3](stxt-core-ref#s4-3)).
		* Effective namespace of the node.
		
		Cardinality is expressed as an optional token in parentheses: `( ... )`.
		It is **independent of the order** of the children (it counts occurrences, not positions).
		
	Subsubheader: 7.1 Allowed forms
	
	Content >>
		Allowed forms:
		
		| Form       | Meaning                      |
		|------------|------------------------------|
		| `num`      | Exactly `num`.               |
		| `*`        | Any number (`0..∞`).         |
		| `+`        | One or more (`1..∞`).        |
		| `?`        | Zero or one (`0..1`).        |
		| `num+`     | `num` or more (`num..∞`).    |
		| `num-`     | Up to `num` (`0..num`).      |
		| `min,max`  | Between `min` and `max`.     |
		
		Rules:
		
		* `num`, `min`, and `max` **MUST** be non-negative integers and **MUST NOT** exceed
		  `4294967295` (2³² − 1), the same bound as `Min`/`Max` in a schema
		  ([*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC* §10](stxt-schema-ref#s10)); a greater value is
		  `CARDINALITY_NOT_VALID`.
		* In `min,max`, `min <= max` **MUST** hold.
		* `+` is equivalent to `1+`.
		* `?` is equivalent to `0,1`.
		* The default cardinality, if omitted, is `*`.
		
	Subsubheader: 7.2 Default cardinality
	
	Content >>
		If cardinality is not specified, the default value is:
		
		* `*` (any number).
		
	Subheader: 8. Types
	
	Content >>
		Types in templates reuse the set of types from @STXT@ Schema, with the same intent: validate the form of the value and,
		optionally, its content.
		
		The type is specified as a word after the cardinality (if present).
		
		Examples:
		
	Code >>
		Date: (1) DATE
		Body: (1) TEXT
		Is Public: (1) BOOLEAN
		
	Content >>
		General rules:
		
		* The explicit type **MUST** match exactly one of the types supported by the implementation.
		* The type **MUST** be written in uppercase (`DATE`, not `date`): the comparison is exact and **CASE-SENSITIVE**.
		* If a line uses a `@Node Name` reference, it **MUST NOT** also declare an explicit type.
		* The semantic rules of types `INLINE`, `GROUP`, `BLOCK`, `TEXT`, `ENUM`, `NUMBER`, `DATE`, etc. **MUST** be consistent with those defined in [*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC*](stxt-schema-ref).
	
	Subsubheader: 8.1 Default type
	
	Content >>
		If the type is omitted, the default type is:
		
		* `INLINE`
		
	Subsubheader: 8.2 Compatibility with children
	
	Content >>
		The compatibility rule with children is the same as in [*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC*](stxt-schema-ref) ([section 9](stxt-schema-ref#s9)):
		
		**Only types `INLINE` and `GROUP` allow children. All others are leaves.**
		
		Therefore:
		
		* If a template node declares children in `Structure >>`, its effective type **MUST** be `INLINE` or `GROUP`. Otherwise, the template **MUST** be considered invalid.
		* The default type `INLINE` allows children, so a node without an explicit type may have children.
		* Any type with content validation (`BLOCK`, `TEXT`, `NUMBER`, `BOOLEAN`, `DATE`, `ENUM`, `BASE64`, etc.) **DOES NOT** allow children.
		
	Subsubheader: 8.3 Type set
	
	Content >>
		The type set is that of [*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC*](stxt-schema-ref), with the same support levels:

		* A template implementation **MUST** accept all the types defined in [*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC*](stxt-schema-ref) ([section 9](stxt-schema-ref#s9), including `MARKDOWN`).
		* The validation requirement of each group is the same as there: it **MUST** validate the
		  structural and basic content types, **SHOULD** validate the extended ones, and **MAY**
		  validate the binary ones.
		
	Subheader: 9. ENUM and value list
	
	Content >>
		If the type is `ENUM`, the template **MUST** declare a list of allowed values, with at least one value.
		
		The value list is specified with brackets `[...]` after the type:
		
		`ENUM [value1, value2, value3]`
		
	Code >>
		Color: (1) ENUM [red, green, blue]
		
	Content >>
		Rules:
		
		* If the type is `ENUM`, the value list **MUST** exist for it to have valid content.
		* If the type is **NOT** `ENUM`, a `[...]` list **MUST** be considered a template error.
		* Values are separated by commas `,`.
		* Trim is applied (spaces/tabs) around each value.
		* Value comparison **MUST** be done on the inline value already normalized through left/right trim.
		* Value comparison **MUST** be exact and **CASE-SENSITIVE**.
		* Comparison **MUST NOT** apply additional canonicalization, removal of diacritics, or normalization equivalent to the canonical name of nodes.
		* At least one value must exist in the list.
		* Values **MUST NOT** be repeated after applying normalization trim.
		
	Subsubheader: 9.1 Limitation of list syntax
		
	Content >>
		Because of the form of the syntax `[a, b, c]`, an `ENUM` value declared in a template **CANNOT**
		contain a comma `,` (value separator) or a closing bracket `]` (end of list).
		It also does not preserve spaces at the beginning or end, which trim removes.
		
		This is a limitation of the **subset expressible by templates**. If `ENUM` values
		with commas, brackets, or significant spaces are needed, a **schema** (`@stxt.schema`) must be used,
		whose `Value` nodes allow any inline value.
		
	Subheader: 10. Namespaces within `Structure`
	
	Content >>
		Each line may include an explicit namespace for the template node:
		
	Code >>
		Metadata (org.example.meta): (?)
		
	Content >>
		Rules:
		
		* If a `Structure` line omits an explicit namespace, it inherits the effective namespace of its parent.
		* If the node has no parent within `Structure`, the default namespace is the template's target namespace.
		* Namespace inheritance works as in @STXT@ data documents.
		* If `(other.ns)` is specified, that template node belongs to that other namespace.
		* If `(other.ns)` is specified and that namespace is different from the target namespace, the line **MUST NOT** define children, explicit type, or `ENUM` values; it **MAY** only declare cardinality.
		
	Subheader: 11. Rules for "defined nodes"
	
	Content >>
		In templates, a node becomes defined by its appearance in `Structure`.
		Unlike Schema, there is no separate `Node:` section; the structure itself is the definition.
		
		Rules:
		
		* Every local node defined in `Structure` becomes part of the template's set of nodes.
		* If a template references cross-namespace nodes, a complete validation system **SHOULD**
		  have the corresponding template or schema for that external namespace if it wishes to deeply validate those nodes.
		* An implementation **MAY** validate only cardinality and structure of the target namespace, treating cross-namespace nodes
		  as a "black box", depending on configuration.
		
	Subheader: 12. Per-node descriptions (`Description`)

	Content >>
		A template **MAY** include a `Description` node, and at most one, in inline (`:`) or block (`>>`) form.

		Its content is not free text describing the template: it is an @STXT@ document whose
		root nodes are **description entries**. Each entry associates a description with a node
		defined in `Structure`:

		* `Node Name: description` — inline entry, for one-line descriptions.
		* `Node Name >>` with the description in its text block — block entry, for multi-line descriptions.

		The correspondence between an entry and its `Structure` node is made by **canonical name**
		([*STXT-SPEC*](stxt-core-ref), [section 4.3](stxt-core-ref#s4-3)). The order of the entries is free: it does not have to follow the order of `Structure`.

		It is the same information that a schema expresses with the `Description` node of each `Node`
		([*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC*](stxt-schema-ref), [section 7.1](stxt-schema-ref#s7-1)); the template groups it in a single block because its
		line syntax does not allow descriptions. Editors **MAY** use these
		descriptions, for example, as contextual help (hover) over the nodes of a document.

	Content >>
		Rules:

		* The content of `Description` **MUST** be valid @STXT@: if it does not parse, the template is invalid.
		* Each entry **MUST** correspond, by canonical name, to a node defined in `Structure`. An entry whose name does not correspond to any defined node **MUST** cause a template error.
		* An entry **MUST NOT** have structured children: it only allows an inline value or a text block.
		* An entry **MUST NOT** declare a namespace different from the template's target namespace: cross-namespace nodes are documented in the template or schema of their own namespace.
		* There **MUST NOT** be more than one entry for the same node.

		Example (short inline entry and multi-line block entry):

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): org.example.tomcat
			Structure >>
				Server (org.example.tomcat):
					Port: (1) INTEGER
					Shutdown command: (?)
			Description >>
				Port: TCP port the server listens on
				Server >>
					Root node of the server configuration:
					it groups ports, commands, and services.

	Subheader: 13. Compilation to Schema (semantic equivalence)
	
	Content >>
		A template can be compiled into an equivalent schema:
		
		* Each local node defined in `Structure` generates a `Node` definition (in the corresponding namespace).
		* The indentation hierarchy in `Structure` generates `Children/Child` in the schema.
		* Cardinality `( ... )` in the template is translated to `Min` / `Max` in the schema.
		* The type is copied to `Type`.
		* `ENUM [a,b]` is translated to `Values/Value`.
		* A `@Node Name` reference reuses the definition already generated for that local node and only modifies the cardinality of the corresponding `Child`. Recursion (self-reference or open ancestor) is translated to a `Child` that points to the same `Node`.
		* A cross-namespace node only generates a `Child` reference; its definition corresponds to the template or schema of that other namespace.
		* Each `Description` entry ([section 12](#s12)) is translated to the `Description` node of the corresponding `Node` in the compiled schema.

		Rules for cardinality translation:
		
		* `(num)` → `Min=num`, `Max=num`
		* `(*)` → without `Min`, without `Max`
		* `(+)` → `Min=1`
		* `(?)` → `Max=1`
		* `(num+)` → `Min=num`
		* `(num-)` → `Max=num`
		* `(min,max)` → `Min=min`, `Max=max`
		
	Subheader: 14. Template Errors
	
	Content >>
		A template is invalid if any of these conditions occurs:
		
		1. The internal indentation of `Structure >>` does not comply with the indentation rules of [*STXT-SPEC*](stxt-core-ref), [section 8](stxt-core-ref#s8) (a mix of tabs and spaces on the same line, non-consecutive levels, spaces that are not multiples of 4, etc.).
		2. The document does not have root `Template (@stxt.template): <target_namespace>`.
		3. `Structure >>` is missing or `Structure` is not a `>>` block.
		4. A non-empty line within `Structure` does not contain `:`.
		5. Malformed cardinality or cardinality with invalid numbers (not non-negative integers, or greater than `4294967295`, [section 7.1](#s7-1)).
		6. Unknown type (according to the set of types supported by the implementation).
		7. `ENUM` type without a `[...]` value list, or with an empty list.
		8. Use of `[...]` if the type is not `ENUM`.
		9. A node with children has an effective type that does not allow children (that is, different from `INLINE` or `GROUP`).
		10. Redefining a local node that has already appeared previously without using `@Node Name` reference.
		11. Using a `@Node Name` reference that does not point to a previous local definition or to an open ancestor.
		12. The name of a `@Node Name` reference does not match (in canonical name) the name of its line.
		13. Declaring both `@Node Name` reference and explicit type on the same line.
		14. Declaring duplicate `ENUM` values after trim normalization, or empty ones (`[a, , b]`, `[a, b,]`).
		15. Defining children, explicit type, or `ENUM` values on a cross-namespace node.
		16. The content of `Description` is not a valid @STXT@ document.
		17. A `Description` entry does not correspond, by canonical name, to any node defined in `Structure`.
		18. A `Description` entry has structured children.
		19. A `Description` entry declares a namespace different from the template's target namespace.
		20. More than one `Description` entry for the same node.
		21. A `@Node Name` reference declares children: the structure is fixed by the referenced definition.
		22. A `@Node Name` reference declares `ENUM` values.

	Content >>
		The validation of a **document** against a template is, by definition, the validation
		against the equivalent compiled schema ([section 13](#s13)): the invalidity conditions of a
		document are those defined in [*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC*](stxt-schema-ref), [section 13](stxt-schema-ref#s13).
		
	Subsubheader: 14.1 Error codes
		
	Content >>
		As in [*STXT-SPEC* §11.1](stxt-core-ref#s11-1), every error carries a stable code, identical
		in every implementation; **from 1.0 on they do not change**. The numbers point to the
		conditions of the list above. A template document is first validated **as a document**
		against the meta-template of [section 16](#s16), so its form errors (a root that is not
		`Template`, a missing or inline `Structure`) come with the codes of
		[*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC* §13.1](stxt-schema-ref#s13-1); the rules of this section are applied
		afterwards.
		
		| Code | Condition |
		|---|---|
		| `INDENTATION_*` codes of *STXT-SPEC* | 1: indentation of `Structure >>` |
		| `TEMPLATE_ROOT_NOT_VALID` | 2: the root is not `Template (@stxt.template): …`, or the target namespace is not valid |
		| `TEMPLATE_NAMESPACE_EMPTY` | 2: the target namespace is empty |
		| `TEMPLATE_MULTIPLE_ROOTS` | the template document has more than one root node, or none |
		| `TEMPLATE_STRUCTURE_REQUIRED` | 3: `Structure >>` missing or not a block |
		| `STRUCTURE_LINE_NOT_VALID` | 4: a `Structure` line written in `>>` form, or whose rule part does not follow [section 6.2](#s6-2) (for instance an unclosed parenthesis); a line with neither `:` nor `>>` is already rejected by the core with `INVALID_LINE` ([*STXT-SPEC* §11.1](stxt-core-ref#s11-1)), at the line of the template |
		| `CARDINALITY_NOT_VALID` | 5: malformed cardinality or invalid numbers |
		| `MIN_GREATER_THAN_MAX` | 5: `(min,max)` with `min` greater than `max` |
		| `TYPE_NOT_VALID` | 6: unknown type |
		| `VALUES_REQUIRED` | 7: `ENUM` without `[...]` or with an empty list |
		| `VALUES_NOT_ALLOWED_FOR_TYPE` | 8: `[...]` on a type other than `ENUM` |
		| `CHILDREN_NOT_ALLOWED_FOR_TYPE` | 9: children under a type that allows none |
		| `REFERENCE_REQUIRED` | 10: second appearance of a local node without `@` |
		| `REFERENCE_NOT_FOUND` | 11: `@Node Name` with no previous definition nor open ancestor |
		| `REFERENCE_NAME_NOT_VALID` | 12: the name after `@` does not match the line's |
		| `REFERENCE_WITH_TYPE_NOT_ALLOWED` | 13: reference and explicit type on the same line |
		| `CHILDREN_NOT_ALLOWED_IN_REFERENCE` | 21: a `@Node Name` reference declares children |
		| `VALUES_NOT_ALLOWED_IN_REFERENCE` | 22: a `@Node Name` reference declares `ENUM` values |
		| `VALUE_DUPLICATED` | 14: repeated `ENUM` values |
		| `VALUE_EMPTY` | 14: an empty `ENUM` value in the list |
		| `CHILDREN_NOT_ALLOWED_IN_EXTERNAL_NAMESPACE` | 15: children on a cross-namespace node |
		| `TYPE_NOT_ALLOWED_IN_EXTERNAL_NAMESPACE` | 15: explicit type on a cross-namespace node |
		| `VALUES_NOT_ALLOWED_IN_EXTERNAL_NAMESPACE` | 15: `ENUM` values on a cross-namespace node |
		| codes of *STXT-SPEC* §11.1 | 16: the content of `Description` does not parse |
		| `DESCRIPTION_NODE_NOT_FOUND` | 17: a `Description` entry with no node in `Structure` |
		| `DESCRIPTION_CHILDREN_NOT_ALLOWED` | 18: a `Description` entry with children |
		| `DESCRIPTION_NOT_ALLOWED_IN_EXTERNAL_NAMESPACE` | 19: a `Description` entry with another namespace |
		| `DESCRIPTION_DUPLICATED` | 20: two `Description` entries for the same node |
		
		The errors of a **document** against a template are those of
		[*STXT-SCHEMA-SPEC* §13.1](stxt-schema-ref#s13-1), because it is validated against the compiled schema.
		
	Subheader: 15. Conformance
	
	Content >>
		A template implementation is conformant if:
		
		* It implements the `Template` and `Structure` grammar of this document.
		* It applies the rules of cardinality, types, `@Node Name` references (including recursion through open ancestor), and `ENUM`.
		* It applies the child compatibility rules by type (only `INLINE` and `GROUP` allow children).
		* It validates cardinalities independently of order.
		* It rejects invalid templates according to [section 14](#s14).
		* It defines a stable template selection criterion (if multiple sources exist),
		  without applying more than one simultaneously per target namespace.
		
	Subheader: 16. Meta-template of the `@stxt.template` system itself
	
	Content >>
		This section defines a recommended minimal template for validating documents in the `@stxt.template` namespace.
		
	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): @stxt.template
			Structure >>
				Template (@stxt.template):
					Description: (?) TEXT
					Structure: (1) BLOCK
		
	Content >>
		Notes:
		
		* `Structure` is `BLOCK` because in templates it must be a `>>` block.
		* `Description` is optional and may be `TEXT`: for the core and for this meta-template its
		  content is opaque text, but it is interpreted with the per-node description grammar
		  of [section 12](#s12), just as the content of `Structure` is interpreted with the template grammar.
		* The `Template` line uses default cardinality (`*`): since the top level of `Structure`
		  has no special root semantics ([section 6](#s6)), no document cardinality is imposed on it.
		
	Subheader: 17. Normative examples

	Subsubheader: 17.1 Simple template (one namespace)

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.docs
			Structure >>
				Document (com.example.docs):
					Title: (1)
					Author: (1)
					Date: (1) DATE
					Body: (1) TEXT
			Description >>
				Document: Simple document with title, author, date, and body
					
	Subsubheader: 17.2 Template with repetition and nested nodes
	
	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.blog.post
			Structure >>
				Post (com.example.blog.post):
					Title: (1)
					Slug: (1)
					Published: (1) BOOLEAN
					Tags: (?)
						Tag: (+)
					Sections: (1)
						Section: (+)
							Heading: (1)
							Content: (1) TEXT
							
	Subsubheader: 17.3 Template with ENUM
	
	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.ui.theme
			Structure >>
				Theme (com.example.ui.theme):
					Name: (1)
					Mode: (1) ENUM [light, dark]
					Accent: (?) ENUM [blue, green, orange]

	Subsubheader: 17.4 Template with local reuse

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): org.example.docs
			Structure >>
				Email (org.example.docs):
					Body Content: (1) TEXT
				Demo (org.example.docs):
					Body Content: (?) @Body Content
					
	Subsubheader: 17.5 Template with recursion

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.doc
			Structure >>
				Document (com.example.doc):
					Title: (1)
					Section: (*)
						Title: (1) @Title
						Text: (?) TEXT
						Section: (*) @Section

	Content >>
		`Section` contains itself at any depth. This is the case that a parser with the
		"previous definition only" rule would incorrectly reject; that is why it is included as a
		normative example of conformance.

	Subsubheader: 17.6 Cross-namespace (external references)
	
	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.docs
			Structure >>
				Document (com.example.docs):
					Metadata (org.example.meta): (?)
					Content: (1) TEXT
					
	Content >>
		In this case:
		
		* `Metadata` belongs to `org.example.meta`.
		* Deep validation of `Metadata` will depend on whether a schema/template exists for `org.example.meta`.
		
	Subheader: 18. Appendix A — Grammar (informal)

	Grammar >>
		TemplateDoc      = "Template" "(" "@stxt.template" ")" ":" NamespaceTarget { TemplateField }
		TemplateField    = DescriptionField | StructureField
		DescriptionField = "Description" ( ":" | ">>" ) DescEntries
		StructureField   = "Structure" ">>" Newline { StructureLine }

		DescEntries      = { DescEntry }
		DescEntry        = NodeName ( ":" Text | ">>" TextBlock )   ; NodeName defined in Structure (section 12)

		StructureLine    = Indent NodeSpec Newline
		NodeSpec         = NodeName [NsOverride] ":" [RuleSpec]
		NsOverride       = "(" ["@"] Namespace ")"
		RuleSpec         = [Card] [NodeRef | Type [EnumValues]]
		
		Card             = "(" CardToken ")"
		CardToken        = "*" | "+" | "?" | Num | Num "+" | Num "-" | Num "," Num
		NodeRef          = "@" NodeName
		Type             = IdentUpper
		EnumValues       = "[" Value { "," Value } "]"
		
		NodeName         = Text up to `(` or `:`, with trim and space compaction
		NamespaceTarget  = Namespace according to STXT-SPEC
		Namespace        = Ident "." Ident { "." Ident }   ; at least 2 Ident
		Ident            = [A-Za-z0-9]+   ; accepted in input; normalized to lowercase (STXT-SPEC section 7)
		IdentUpper       = [A-Z0-9_]+     ; types are written in uppercase; exact comparison (section 8)
		Value            = any text without "," or "]", with trim   ; see section 9.1
		
